t(52, 1595, """After the case of the Egyptian citizen, the petroleum engineer in Qatar who spent his youth working and registered a great deal of money to his (only) son, then was thrown out by his son, the Ummah must pay attention to the following:

There are strong fiqh bases on which legal and legislative ijtihad in Islamic countries can rely to prevent or restrict registering properties to children during one's lifetime, to protect parents from ingratitude and to guarantee a natural and just inheritance after death.

The most important fiqh tools and solutions that can be codified and put into application:

1. Codifying "the right to revoke a gift"
* The fiqh basis: the majority of jurists (Shafi'is, Hanbalis and Malikis) hold that the father and mother have the full right to revoke a gift they gave their children and to reclaim their property at any time.
* The shar'i basis: the Prophet's ﷺ saying: «It is not lawful for a man to give a gift or make a donation and then take it back, except the father in what he gives his child».
* The legal application: the legislator can draft a law preventing the registration of any transfer or gift from a father to his children as "final and irrevocable"; rather it is registered legally as a "conditional gift", or revocable at the father's request without need for the son's consent.

2. Stipulating "a lifetime right of usufruct" ('umra)
* The fiqh basis: Islamic fiqh permits what is known as "'umra", or stipulating the benefit.
* The legal application: legislation can impose a binding text in gift and transfer contracts between relatives requiring that the father retain "the right of usufruct, residence and use" of the transferred property (such as a villa or a car) for his lifetime, and actual possession or the right of sale passes to the son only after the father's death. This legally prevents the son from expelling his father or selling the property.

3. Prohibition to block the pretext of undutifulness and injustice (sadd al-dhara'i')""")

t(53, 60, """* The fiqh basis: the rule of "blocking the means" (prohibiting permissible means if they lead to a certain or likely harm).
* The legal application: since transferring all wealth to the children during one's lifetime has in many cases led to the harm of parents being expelled and treated with undutifulness, those in authority (the legislator) have the right to restrict this permissible act by law. A law can be enacted preventing the father from giving up his only dwelling, or more than a certain proportion of his property (a third, for example), during his lifetime, to avoid leaving himself destitute.""")

h(53, 700, """The cases have multiplied and repeated, and the matter cannot be kept silent about!""")

t(53, 1320, """If Muslims are in constant worry, each carrying his worry that grows every day, how will their souls have room for one another?! This is how people complain today of the severing of kinship ties, the corruption of relationships and the disintegration of society. For when people saw with their eyes the fangs of global capitalism, and that global capitalism sank its fangs into their necks, every person came to carry a worry like a great mountain, and a person's soul grew narrow toward his own self; so how can this person's soul have room for people?! Except those whom Allah guided, who were patient with the trial of global capitalism and knew their Lord, so their Lord chose them and purified them: a purified, chosen male servant, and a purified, selected female servant. Global capitalism shook nothing in them; they are firm and deep-rooted. But how many are they among people? They are like the single white hair on the body of a huge bull. As for the rest of the people around them, narrowness of soul, which has reached its limit in shattering souls, snatches them away. For global capitalism was the engine of all this corruption on earth: so the earth was corrupted and souls were corrupted; it corrupted people's lives, their livelihood, their security, their happiness, their worship and their hope in tomorrow, and smashed them without mercy or pity, and did not spare a single vein pulsing in them so as to leave them sound and well; rather it crushed them, made them bleed, humiliated them, bit them to pieces, wiped out the blessing from their lives, and turned every beautiful thing Allah the Exalted created for them and in them into calamity, pain and suffering.""")

h(53, 2200, """The souls of the Muslims grew narrow toward their own selves, so they grew narrow toward one another!""")

t(54, 355, """Our Lord, You have given us of everything by making us know Your oneness, and You gladdened our hearts that You are One, Unique, Single, Self-Sufficient, with no partner, no rival, no likeness, no child and no consort. So we were happy through You, our Lord, and we rejoiced with great joy when we knew You are You, and when we knew that we worship You and humble ourselves to You, and rub our noses and foreheads in the dust for You on earth. You honoured us and raised our standing when our faces prostrated to You with the prostration of the humbly devoted worshippers. You honoured us with our lowliness before You, and raised us by our need of You, and blessed us by teaching us how to rub our noses in the dust for You, prostrating and humbled, lowly before Your lordship and divinity, sanctifying Your exalted Self, submitting to Your decrees that rule over us as the Lord's rule over the servant. You made us understand by Your kindness, and made us know by Your beautiful love, that all You wrote for us before You created us is good for us, and that all we receive of the ruler of Your decrees concerning us is for our benefit, and that whoever gives thanks is rewarded, and whoever is displeased loses. O Allah, make firm the hearts of Your servants upon Your purposes, be kind to their weakness if they are ignorant of Your rulings, and make their world a bridge of well-being until they reach the abode of Your permanence.

O Allah, amen, O Lord, and for our offspring, our loved ones, our families, and the Muslim men and Muslim women""")

h(54, 1200, """O Allah, to You belongs praise, for You are Allah, the Lord, the Creator, the Mighty, the One, the King besides whom there is no god and no lord but You""")

